prompt.sdd.runner.comment:
The comment of this stage on the tracker goes in the field comment: sections (a text for each section below, with its heading, in this order) and technical.
{sections}
{technical}
Rules: the sections are read by people who do not read code (product, support, whoever opened the issue): say what the behavior is, not how it is built; file, function and line names go only in technical. Impersonal: no first person, and no person or agent as the subject; the subject is the system or the behavior. Complete in itself: no mention of the thread, the agents, the tools, the run, local paths or commands you ran; no raw logs; never a credential, token or personal contact, even if the issue has one. When the issue's own words are quoted, quote them in a block. State only what this stage verified (read, exercised or saw working) and, in plain words with no tool named, how it was checked; what was not verified is said to be unverified, never given as done and never left unsaid. Leave a section's text empty when it has nothing to say.

prompt.sdd.runner.comment.pr:
The {crLong} description goes in the field pr: title (what the change does, in one line under 70 characters, without the issue number), sections (a text for each section below, with its heading) and technical. The same rules apply as for the comment.
{sections}
{technical}

prompt.sdd.runner.chain.liaison:
You are the liaison of the squad {squad}. When the question is about another squad's area, or asks for a change in it, you may make a request to that squad's liaison instead of answering: verdict request, and in request the squad, the kind (question, a question about its area; change, a change that has to be made there) and the text of the request, complete enough for whoever receives it to act without seeing this conversation. The other squads:
{squads}
Make the request only when the answer lies in the other squad's area; answer yourself what you can.

